1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,187
Advanced packaging.

2
00:00:02,137 --> 00:00:05,737
This is episode four of From Sand to GPU.

3
00:00:05,737 --> 00:00:10,650
After the front-end fab, an AI GPU die is not a finished product.

4
00:00:10,650 --> 00:00:15,512
It still has to meet high-bandwidth memory and leave as an integrated package.

5
00:00:15,512 --> 00:00:22,062
That step is two-point-five-D and three-D advanced packaging — the class of flows often called CoWoS at TSMC.

6
00:00:22,062 --> 00:00:27,187
At a high level, known-good logic dies and known-good HBM stacks are prepared.

7
00:00:27,187 --> 00:00:33,750
Dies attach to a silicon interposer or to an organic redistribution layer with local silicon bridges.

8
00:00:33,750 --> 00:00:39,775
The assembly bonds to an organic package substrate, often an ABF-based flip-chip BGA.

9
00:00:39,775 --> 00:00:43,025
Then the package goes to a board or module.

10
00:00:43,025 --> 00:00:46,825
TSMC’s CoWoS brand covers several variants.

11
00:00:46,825 --> 00:00:53,950
CoWoS-S uses a classic silicon interposer — powerful, but reticle-size limits bite hard on giant packages.

12
00:00:53,950 --> 00:01:03,025
CoWoS-L uses organic RDL with local silicon interconnect bridges, a path industry analysis calls likely for multi-die NVIDIA GPUs.

13
00:01:03,025 --> 00:01:07,525
CoWoS-R is an RDL-focused variant, product-dependent.

14
00:01:07,525 --> 00:01:13,887
Confirmed: CoWoS is TSMC’s platform for integrating logic with HBM in AI and HPC.

15
00:01:13,887 --> 00:01:19,850
Exact CoWoS SKU for every product is not always a NVIDIA one-liner — label that carefully.

16
00:01:19,850 --> 00:01:27,250
SoIC-style bonding sits adjacent: finer-pitch three-D stacking that may combine with two-point-five-D HBM integration.

17
00:01:27,250 --> 00:01:33,500
Exact SoIC use on Vera Rubin is unknown without NVIDIA or TSMC primary confirmation.

18
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:43,787
Inputs include logic and HBM known-good die, interposer wafers or bridge dies, RDL materials, microbumps or hybrid bonds, underfill, and the package substrate.

19
00:01:43,787 --> 00:01:47,862
Thermal interface materials show up later at module level.

20
00:01:47,862 --> 00:01:50,412
Capacity is concentrated.

21
00:01:50,412 --> 00:01:59,462
TSMC advanced-packaging sites in Taiwan — Longtan, Taichung, Zhunan, Chiayi, and Tainan expansions in industry press — carry the HPC narrative.

22
00:01:59,462 --> 00:02:04,337
Treat specific site lists as likely until verified against TSMC.

23
00:02:04,337 --> 00:02:14,050
OSAT players such as Amkor and ASE grow advanced packaging, but NVIDIA HPC has historically been CoWoS-centric — again, likely, not a universal rule.

24
00:02:14,050 --> 00:02:25,337
Equipment classes include chip-on-wafer bonders, wafer thinning and TSV reveal, RDL patterning, flip-chip bonders, underfill, and inspection such as X-ray and scanning acoustic microscopy.

25
00:02:25,337 --> 00:02:31,462
Yield risk scales with package size: warpage, thermo-mechanical stress, and microbump yield.

26
00:02:31,462 --> 00:02:38,887
CoWoS-L trades giant-interposer yield risk for bridge-plus-RDL complexity — that trade is a likely analyst narrative.

27
00:02:38,887 --> 00:02:49,887
Lead times of fifty-two to seventy-eight weeks for CoWoS slots appear repeatedly in secondary twenty-twenty-six trackers — estimated, useful as a signal, not a hard TSMC primary fact.

28
00:02:49,887 --> 00:02:55,250
Without CoWoS-class capacity, front-end wafers sit as unfinished inventory.

29
00:02:55,250 --> 00:03:01,562
This is where a GPU becomes an AI accelerator package — multi-die logic plus HBM stacks.

30
00:03:01,562 --> 00:03:15,637
Trackers should watch TSMC earnings commentary on CoWoS capacity, equipment move-in at advanced-packaging sites, estimated NVIDIA allocation share in the press, package-size rumors that stress substrates and yield, and competing OSAT offerings.

31
00:03:15,637 --> 00:03:18,937
That is episode four: advanced packaging.

32
00:03:18,937 --> 00:03:25,287
Next time: substrates, HBM, and interposers — the three supply chains that converge on the package.

